m and maintains the **row relation** of §6.1 (the `$ast` relation `q` runs over) from the rows it observes. `dao.jing` is the content store the rows already live in; it is not the indexer's output; code on a stream is rows end to end; `t` and `m` live in a ledger of\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:52:|                | :segment/sha256-…]             | code or record address: names,      | in a `dao.space`   | (§8), transacted by a    | realized by ledger records and |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:53:|                |                                | history, retraction, derivation,    | ledger             | `dao.space` transactor   | the refs that publish them     |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:95:| indexed storage        | the transactor, btree, and covered indexes, `pull`/entity navigation, attribute-dimension queries (`§6.5`): datom-native machinery |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:231:|         | metadata                         |                                                                                                                           |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:360:- **Source positions** and any frontend metadata (`:yang/*` keys, Clojure\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:361:  reader metadata on symbols). Tracked instead in secondary side tables.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:432:| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:566:`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized, metadata-aware hand printer\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:578:| `[:literal ^{:a 1} [1 2]]` and `[:literal ^{:a 2} [1   | distinct                            | `data` admits plain metadata (`linearize.cljc:58`) and programs may read |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:579:| 2]]`                                                   |                                      | it; collection metadata is now address-significant                      |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:583:contract precisely and its remaining residuals: scalar (symbol) metadata\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:597:metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to emit\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:642:**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:669:recursive Datalog rule over the row relation itself, needing no separate\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:908:### 6.1 The row relation and the two directions around it\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:922:**The Dedicated AST Indexer:** This row relation (called `$ast` in queries) is maintained by the dedicated AST indexer (§1). The indexer is a `dao.stream` observer peer to the evaluators (§7.1); its input is the row batches of §7.1; its output is this row relation plus the occurrence relation below; it is distinct from `dao.space.index`, which indexes datoms only (§6.5); it is a projection keeper per §1's Query layer, so discarding it loses nothing.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1161:shapes (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte arrays), not a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1289:;; design specifies (§9.1: lower must be adapted to read the row relation\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1501:hold a foreign reference (`datom.md:140-180`). The transactor enforces\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1504:(`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:74-90`) rejects a caller-supplied `t`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1512:| `t`          | the datom `t` the transactor stamped                                                                                                                    |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1523:row; anything written as `[e a v t m]` is transactor input, with `e` a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1586:metadata-entity mechanism, an id ≥ 16 carrying `:db/op` and provenance\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1587:(`datom.cljc:2-7`, `datom.md:172-180`; the transactor's map-`m` input at\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1588:`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:15-29`). A fact whose `m` names a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1589:metadata entity is live under the current fold, which removes only\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1593:a metadata entity's `:db/op` for Datomic-visible validity is the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1664:enforced by how the transactor resolves ids: `apply-tempid-map` replaces a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1666:reference attribute** (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:168-185`); a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1672:  `:yin/source-batch`, which no transactor resolves, as today;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1677:  the transactor within the log medium exactly as `:yin/source-node` is\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1698:`program-out`, and the log. The local event id remains log-local, as\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1726:  the history it is handed (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:189-207`),\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1728:  `transact!` allocates only `t` under its lock and appends datoms that\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1729:  are already resolved (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:23